# PART 2 — REBUILD THE GRAMMAR CURRICULUM USING EFFECTIVE LEARNING PRINCIPLES

## 2.1 Learning philosophy

Build the curriculum around evidence-informed language-learning approaches.

Use:

- Comprehensible input.
- Structured scaffolding.
- Explicit grammar explanations when useful.
- Active recall.
- Spaced repetition.
- Retrieval practice.
- Interleaved review.
- Contextual vocabulary acquisition.
- Communicative language teaching.
- Task-based learning.
- Gradual difficulty progression.
- Immediate corrective feedback.
- Repeated exposure to high-utility vocabulary.
- Meaningful reading and listening practice.
- Opportunities for productive language use.

Do not assume one teaching method is universally best for every student or linguistic skill.

Combine complementary approaches where appropriate.

The main objective is to help students understand, remember, and actually use Ukrainian.

## 2.2 Restructure the curriculum by proficiency

Use CEFR principles to organize learning progression.

Treat A0 as an introductory pre-A1 stage rather than an official CEFR level.

### A0 — Absolute Beginner

- Ukrainian alphabet.
- Cyrillic reading.
- Pronunciation fundamentals.
- Word stress.
- Greetings.
- Introducing yourself.
- Formal versus informal communication.
- Basic pronouns.
- Simple sentence patterns.
- Yes/no questions.
- Question words.
- Basic negation.
- Essential everyday expressions.

### A1 — Beginner

- Noun gender.
- Common nouns.
- Singular and plural.
- Present-tense verbs.
- Basic adjective agreement.
- Everyday conversations.
- Expressing likes and wants.
- Common prepositions.
- Introductory grammatical cases.
- Family, food, work, hobbies, and daily activities.
- Basic past tense.

### A2 — Elementary

- Expanded noun declension.
- More complete pronoun and adjective inflection.
- Past and future tense.
- Verb aspect.
- Common motion verbs.
- Directions and transportation.
- Travel and daily errands.
- Comparisons.
- Sentence connectors.
- More complex conversations.

### B1 — Intermediate

- More sophisticated case usage.
- Detailed verb aspect.
- Motion verbs and prefixes.
- Longer narratives.
- Complex sentence patterns.
- Conditional expressions.
- Natural conversation strategies.
- Increased reading comprehension.

### B2–C1 — Upper Intermediate and Advanced

- Complex grammatical structures.
- Advanced syntax.
- Nuanced aspect and word choice.
- Idiomatic expressions.
- Stylistic variation.
- More advanced listening and reading.
- Abstract discussions.
- Natural extended communication.

Do not assign CEFR levels based solely on vocabulary counts or an arbitrary lesson sequence.

Ensure each lesson introduces concepts only after the necessary prerequisites have been taught.

